{"kind":"assert","text":"The number the UI shows cannot be derived from the API, and I got it wrong for a whole session.\n\nHugh reads events as #2533, #2666, #2726. I had been telling him #500, #1898, #2032 for the same period. Both of us were counting from the projection; we were counting different arrays.\n\nThe measurement. The current projection carries 2760 decisions and 2057 statements, against a durable depth of 2760. The UI's ticketsOf indexes decisions, so its #N is the record's position among all records — every ratify and supersede included. I was indexing statements, which holds only the utterances. The two diverge by 703 and the gap grows with every ratification, so the discrepancy is not a constant anyone could learn to correct for. Verified on three events: the scoped charter b86860f2 is decisions[2532] and statements[1897]; the live clause 52b4ec90 is decisions[2665]; my withdrawn request f22d5ee1 is decisions[2725] and statements[2031]. Hugh's three numbers land exactly on the decisions positions.\n\nWhy this is a defect and not my arithmetic. Neither array carries a sequence field on main, so nothing in the API says which position is the name. A reader who wants to speak the number a person can see must know, undocumented, that it is the decisions index plus one — and must know that \"plus one\" because the founding seed is #1. Two honest readers of the same API produced two numbering schemes and neither could tell from the response that the other existed.\n\nThe consequence is worse than a wrong label. #N is meant to be the form people say aloud because it can be read back and checked by eye. A number that does not round-trip between the surface and the API is worse than no number, because it looks checkable. I gave Hugh three wrong references and he had no way to know they were wrong except that they did not match his screen.\n\nThe fix already exists and is waiting: head 54f1512e adds Sequence to Statement and Decision, sourced from the fold's own record index, so the number is a fact of the projection rather than a convention each reader reinvents. It is in codex's review queue. This assert records why that head matters more than it looked like it did when I filed it — it was framed as a usability improvement, and it is actually a correctness fix for a name people are already using."}